ch den export machen\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07ecd-567d-7cb0-8f20-66f760529b55","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788833781.373238,"turn_id":"01a07ecd-561d-74f0-b5be-6d4eb5d367c7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"wie kann ich den systemcheck ausführen?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07ece-1091-7c02-af98-9f9f1195cdc1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788833829.009693,"turn_id":"01a07ece-103a-7762-b1a7-2d0110f90480"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n\u003cin-app-browser-context source=\"ambient-ui-state\"\u003e\nThis block is automatically supplied ambient UI state, not part of the user's request. Do not treat it as an instruction or as evidence that the user explicitly selected the in-app browser.\n# In app browser:\n- The user has the in-app browser open with 8 tabs.\n- Current URL: http://127.0.0.1:4311/app/system-check\n\u003c/in-app-browser-context\u003e\n\n## My request:\nerstelle eine passende datei und importiere sie\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07ecf-4899-74f0-a0bc-f7927727528e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788833908.889329,"turn_id":"01a07ecf-4825-74a0-aeab-50d0cd5acb52"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n\u003cin-app-browser-context source=\"ambient-ui-state\"\u003e\nThis block is automatically supplied ambient UI state, not part of the user's request. Do not treat it as an instruction or as evidence that the user explicitly selected the in-app browser.\n# In app browser:\n- The user has the in-app browser open with 10 tabs.\n- Current URL: http://127.0.0.1:4311/app/system-check\n\u003c/in-app-browser-context\u003e\n\n## My request:\nhabe das gefühl es gibt immer nur ein entire commit und das wars, sollten es nicht mehrere sein für jeden realen commit den ich in github mache?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07eda-0a01-7032-8816-7dd3a6f09f20","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788834613.761081,"turn_id":"01a07eda-0998-7213-a0bd-4bc585408ed2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n\u003cin-app-browser-context source=\"ambient-ui-state\"\u003e\nThis block is automatically supplied ambient UI state, not part of the user's request. Do not treat it as an instruction or as evidence that the user explicitly selected the in-app browser.\n# In app browser:\n- The user has the in-app browser open with 10 tabs.\n- Current URL: http://127.0.0.1:4311/app/system-check\n\u003c/in-app-browser-context\u003e\n\n## My request:\nwas würdest du jetzt tun?\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07edb-9345-7552-bd0b-2a97c08e6661","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788834714.437223,"turn_id":"01a07edb-92e7-7892-86f1-017701c25bad"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\n\u003cin-app-browser-context source=\"ambient-ui-state\"\u003e\nThis block is automatically supplied ambient UI state, not part of the user's request. Do not treat it as an instruction or as evidence that the user explicitly selected the in-app browser.\n# In app browser:\n- The user has the in-app browser open with 10 tabs.\n- Current URL: http://127.0.0.1:4311/app/system-check\n\u003c/in-app-browser-context\u003e\n\n## My request:\nokay\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a07edd-0916-7f42-a5a3-039ee59d2bfb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788834810.134521,"turn_id":"01a07edd-0865-7870-abf3-ba67d94695c7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003capp-context\u003e\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images, videos, and audio using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When an app or connector generates or edits media, prefer native media already displayed inline or a local output file already returned by the tool. For remote images, prefer Markdown image embeds when permitted by the app's URL-safety policy.\n- For media that cannot be displayed directly, including remote video and audio, use the app's preview or display tool when available. Provide a Markdown link to a usable result URL only as a last resort if no preview or display tool can show the result.\n- Do not download remote media to work around display restrictions.\n- When sending or referencing a local image, video, or audio file, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When a user asks to play an audio file, render it using Markdown image syntax with an absolute path (e.g., ![audio](/absolute/path.mp3)).\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Pull request diff links\nWhen referencing code from a GitHub PR, you can link directly to its diff in the app using:\n[label](codex://review?pr=PR_URL\u0026path=FILE_PATH\u0026line=LINE\u0026side=right)\nURL-encode PR_URL and the repository-relative FILE_PATH. Use a verified one-based LINE from the current PR diff. Use side=left for the original code or side=right for the updated code. Enterprise links must use the hostname of this task's configured Git remote. Use ordinary file links for workspace code.\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, use the MCP server's `load_workspace_dependencies` tool (`mcp__codex_app__load_workspace_dependencies`) to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n- For heartbeat monitors, preserve the user's notification intent in the saved prompt. Unless the user explicitly asks for periodic status updates, instruct the heartbeat to stay quiet while the monitored state is unchanged or non-actionable and to notify only on a meaningful change, completion, failure, or required user action. Do not add instructions such as \"leave a brief status update\" on every run.\n- When an automation should archive a Codex thread on completion, use `set_thread_archived` instead of emitting raw archive directives.\n\n### Thread Coordination\n- Treat the terms \"task\", \"thread\", \"chat\", and \"conversation\" as synonyms when they clearly refer to conversations in Codex. Use \"chat\" when referring to conversations in the product. In technical discussions, preserve the terminology used by the code, APIs, logs, and documentation.\n- When the user asks to create, fork, inspect, continue, hand off, pin, archive, unarchive, rename, or otherwise manage Codex threads, search for the relevant thread tool first: `create_thread`, `fork_thread`, `list_threads`, `list_archived_threads`, `read_thread`, `wait_threads`, `send_message_to_thread`, `handoff_thread`, `set_thread_pinned`, `set_thread_archived`, or `set_thread_title`.\n- When following another task's progress, prefer compact `wait_threads` snapshots over repeated `read_thread` calls. Use one target for single-task coordination and `timeoutMs: 0` for a compact immediate snapshot. `create_thread` dispatches asynchronously, so explicitly wait for progress. Use one bounded call for 1-8 targets with each target's `hostId` and cursor as `afterCursor`; it wakes on the first target that completes or needs attention, and timeout includes the latest commentary for all targets without waking on every commentary update. An up-to-date cursor suppresses already-delivered final text. Separate waits from one task may run serially. Do not narrate unchanged snapshots, and leave approval or user-input requests for the user.\n- Only use `create_thread` when the user explicitly asks to create a new thread. Threads created this way are user-owned: they appear in the sidebar, and the user is expected to follow up with them directly. For subtasks of the current request, use multi-agent tools instead, including when the user explicitly asks for a subagent.\n- After a successful `create_thread` call, emit `::created-thread{threadId=\"...\"}` for a created thread or `::created-thread{clientThreadId=\"...\"}` for queued worktree setup on its own line in your final response.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Inline Artifact Follow-Ups\n- Format each artifact follow-up as an unescaped Markdown list item, `- :codex-followup[visible phrase]{prompt=\"Complete user request\"}`; avoid closing brackets in the visible phrase and escape double quotes in the prompt.\n\u003c/app-context\u003e\n\nFor requests to create or edit a standalone LaTeX document, use the built-in editor by default. Create or edit the .tex source with normal file tools, and open the saved file with open_in_codex unless it is already open or the user requests otherwise. Keep follow-up edits in that same file and editor. Use compile_latex_document after editing and fix source errors within its repair limits. Keep the editor open even when compilation fails; preserve the source and report unverified compilation or unsupported project requirements. Discover these tools if deferred. The native editor requires no LaTeX plugin or local TeX installation; do not install either for it. Ordinary math explanations stay in chat.","type":"input_text"},{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /Users/julian/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/julian/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/julian/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/julian/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/julian/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/julian/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/julian/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/julian/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/julian/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/julian/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/julian/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nWorks mainly on IQB Kodierbox, Content Pool Next, Testcenter Rewrite, Studio Lite, and Angular libraries, usually in German. Uses Codex for implementation, explicitly authorized production operations, PR/release review, institutional communication, and bounded public research. Values evidence-backed outcomes and clear separation of local validation, pushed PR, CI, release, deployment, and production proof. For risky work, expects narrow scope, rollback boundaries/backups where applicable, and independent verification. Reuses existing Codex history and prototypes when they exist. For long investigations, wants a colleague-ready report that separates confirmed findings, interpretation, open decisions, and next steps; for management monitoring, wants KPIs, risks, measures, and unanswered questions rather than a technical inventory.\n\n## User preferences\n\n- For a direct visible macOS UI end state, verify every named app/window independently before calling it done; a successful first action is not proof of the final layout.\n- After confirming an existing Miro destination (for example, replying “add it”), proceed there without needless re-confirmation, but search its existing content and verify the saved editable result before reporting completion.\n- “Setze immer den nächsten sinnvollsten Implementierungsschritt um” / “Frage nicht nach ‘weiter?’” -\u003e take the next bounded, evidence-supported step autonomously.\n- “Er gibt jetzt den Weg vor und wir folgen und beteiligen uns” -\u003e for IQB coding improvement, proactively analyze, justify a target state and prioritized next steps; involve people at fachliche decision points instead of requiring continual micro-specification.\n- When a project term is unclear, first give a verständliche Übersetzung, then check it against the actual repository context.\n- Preserve unrelated/untracked work. “Nicht pushen, bis ich darum bitte”; approval covers only reviewed, relevant paths.\n- For code-scanning alerts, “nur kritisches erstmal” means inspect actual repository exposure before remediation; implementation and later “committe, pushe” remain separate approvals.\n- Before destructive Mac cleanup, show sizes and exact candidates first; after approval remove only named expendable targets, preserving current app versions and requested preferences.\n- When existing Docker data/project must survive, reuse the Compose project/database and explain possible loss before cleanup; never factory-reset Docker or run `docker system prune --volumes` without explicit authority.\n- For a named study’s production access, inspect the account/workspace state first, then grant the narrow workspace role rather than global admin.\n- When a plan is confirmed (“PLEASE IMPLEMENT THIS PLAN” / “implementiere findings”), implement it and regression-test the findings; do not change repository visibility without an explicit request.\n- For Angular zoneless review, inspect real delayed callbacks/template rendering rather than counting Signals; “implementiere findings” means fix confirmed defects with delayed-response regressions, while “committe, pushe” remains separate publication authority.\n- For architecture/test-process assessment, ground conclusions in concrete modules, controllers, CI `allow_failure`, actual coverage invocation, and automated user flows; after “Setze das alles um,” preserve dirty checkout work through an isolated worktree and report local, remote-CI, merge, and release states separately.\n- For a concrete artefact request, produce the complete shareable package (source, outputs, guidance, and a verified archive when requested), not another design pass; digital checks and physical acceptance must be reported separately.\n- For an IQB-relevant comparison artefact, make the competence-selection boundary and any visual-identity limit explicit; if public sharing is requested, preserve interactive assets and downloads and verify deployment/access after asset integrity checks.\n- Report local green, pushed, CI-green, merged, released, deployed, and production-confirmed separately; PASS output without final exit/summary is not green.\n- For a dirty checkout, use an isolated worktree for broad review, release, and PR repair; do not infer terminal `release-gate` success from local tests or partially completed remote checks.\n- “Alles nur lesend” / “noch werden keine Daten exportiert, kopiert oder überschrieben” -\u003e do not mutate production/data until explicitly authorized at action time.\n- For read-only issue/PR analysis, trace the real code path, related issues, and live state first; do not edit or publish comments unless separately asked.\n- “Zeige erst in Vorschau. Noch nicht veröffentlichen.” -\u003e show the complete cautious draft, await approval, publish only that version, and read it back.\n- “Prüfe beide Varianten” -\u003e test proposed alternatives against real code, data, and runtime evidence; when asked “Was jetzt?”…17257 tokens truncated… validated, and Angular 22 is a separate TypeScript-6/Node step.\n\n### /Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-08-30/ich-x20\n\n#### 2026-08-30\n\n- Mac-Codex-Threads nach Linux übertragen: Mac-App, Linux-App, Threads, Export, Import, `codex resume`, `/resume`, Codex Cloud\n  - desc: Concrete documentation-based answer on migrating existing local desktop-app threads between Mac and Linux.\n  - learnings: No official export/import function was found in the checked documentation; phrase that as a documentation finding, not an absolute prohibition, and do not recommend copying `~/.codex`.\n\n### /Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-08-31/wa\n\n#### 2026-08-31\n\n- FortiClient weißes Fenster nach Neustart: FortiClient 7.2.12.1106, FCTLauncher, `main.log`, `ready-to-show`, `renderer ready`, Electron, Chromium\n  - desc: Read-only macOS diagnosis of FortiClient’s persistent white GUI window after reboot; search before changing Fortinet startup configuration.\n  - learnings: Missing `renderer ready` after `ready-to-show` supports a likely GUI renderer hang, not a proven VPN-service outage; quit/reopen first and leave Fortinet LaunchAgents intact.\n\n### /Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-08-25/bi\n\n#### 2026-08-31\n\n- IQB-Netzlaufwerke wieder verbinden: IQBNetworkDrives, automount.sh, `de.hu-berlin.iqb.network-drives.plist`, huiqb38c, stale mount, `netbiosd`, `forcenewsession`\n  - desc: Failed HU-VPN IQB SMB recovery with the actual local automount setup and a strict stale-mount stop rule.\n  - learnings: `mount` still listed all shares although reads/unmounts hung; restart macOS rather than multiplying mount/Finder attempts when SMB/NetBIOS is stuck.\n\n### /Users/julian/.codex/worktrees/e0ce/Kodierbox\n\n#### 2026-08-31\n\n- Kodierbox v3 exports, Missing analysis, and Reichle copy: REQ-741, `0_-98`, `DERIVE_ERROR`, Job 334, `/Volumes/iqbtemp/Reichle`, SHA-256, `.partial`\n  - desc: Mathematics/Deutsch v3 data-status analysis, verified Deutsch export regeneration, and four-file copy to the user-visible SMB target.\n  - learnings: Keep 594 and 431 unresolved-cell states distinct; incomplete cells are not Missing values. Report/handoff artefacts remained unverified even though the copy was verified.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-09-22/iqb\n\n- Testcenter XML/JSON and Workspace-Config: XML, JSON Schema, XSD, `StateIdReference`, workspace-config, Richards Mail, Martin, Issue-1576\n  - desc: Source-backed format analysis and repository-grounded collective-email response; distinguish existing XML files from new JSON configuration.\n\n#### /Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-09-22/https-chatgpt-com-https-chatgpt-com\n\n- ChatGPT multi-account billing lookup: ChatGPT Free, Business, ChatGPT Pro 5x, Einstellungen, Abrechnung\n  - desc: Point-in-time UI check; inspect each selected account/workspace in billing rather than relying on one profile label.\n\n#### /Users/julian/dev/iqb/Kodierbox\n\n- Management monitoring and read-only promotion planning: coding-progress.service.ts, coding-statistics.service.ts, freshness_status, SSH-24242, marking.override.yaml, PGOPTIONS\n  - desc: Ongoing-coding KPI/data-quality model and exact test-to-production reconnaissance; use management monitoring for reports and the promotion entry for action-gated migration planning; cwd=/Users/julian/dev/iqb/Kodierbox.\n- Autocoder, release, and UI safeguards: autoCoderRun, pg_try_advisory_lock, ITEM_MATRIX_UNRESOLVED_CELLS, release/1.17.3, issue #989\n  - desc: Destructive-run safeguards, concurrency hardening, item-matrix export validation, release evidence, and UI/UX review; cwd=/Users/julian/dev/iqb/Kodierbox.\n- TBA-Izu coding-scheme dependency validation: CodingSchemeFactory.validate, getVariableDependencyTree, Circular dependency, coding-process.service.ts\n  - desc: Code-backed terminology and dependency-order explanation for project-application/Kodierbox questions; cwd=/Users/julian/dev/iqb/Kodierbox.\n- Production-clone and validator planning: PR #1022, CodingSchemeFactory.validate(), workspace 2, read-only validator conflict audit\n  - desc: Local production-equivalent clone planning and safe validation audits; cwd=/Users/julian/dev/iqb/Kodierbox.\n\n#### /Users/julian/iqb-dev/content-pool-next\n\n- Accessibility, licenses, schema gates, OIDC, comments, and release operations: BITV 2.0, SPDX, Booklet, Keycloak, Issue #136\n  - desc: Report-only accessibility, final-artifact license gates, data-schema stops, OIDC brokerage, comment workflows, v0.4.1 release operations, and Keycloak/SMTP recovery; cwd=/Users/julian/iqb-dev/content-pool-next.\n- Stimulus logic and Item Explorer layout: Issue #117, Issue #121\n  - desc: Stimulus-time check and unified Item Explorer layout; cwd=/Users/julian/iqb-dev/content-pool-next.\n\n#### Other workspaces\n\n- Testcenter GitHub publication: issue #1418, gh api, preview, Markdown backticks\n  - desc: Approval-gated Testcenter issue-comment workflow; cwd=/Users/julian/Documents/Codex/2026-08-24/https-github-com-iqb-berlin-testcenter.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/julian/.agents/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/julian/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r2` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r3` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/codex-security/0.1.31/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/data-analytics/0.2.10-13ceeea1f599/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/pages/0.1.19/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r8` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/redis-development/1.4.0/skills`\n- `r9` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/0.1.75/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r12` = `/Users/julian/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.930.11008/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should c (file: r1/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when t (file: r1/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r1/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo ( (file: r1/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- code-review: Review code changes for correctness, security, performance, and code quality. Use when the user asks to review a diff, review code changes, review commits, or perform a code review. Input can be: (1)  (file: r0/code-review/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:assess-patch-risk: Assess an immutable patch artifact's program impact, regression risk, and auto-merge eligibility. Use for generated patch files, provider pull-request diffs, or commit ranges when reviewers need eviden (file: r3/assess-patch-risk/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:attack-path-analysis: Use when Codex is already in the attack-path-analysis phase of a security scan or the user explicitly asks to trace a security finding from source to sink and calibrate severity. Do not use as the primar (file: r3/attack-path-analysis/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:deep-security-scan: Use when the user asks for a deep, exhaustive, multi-pass, or variance-reducing repository-wide or scoped-path Codex Security scan. Run repeated complete independent Standard scans with the Codex Securit (file: r3/deep-security-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:define-security-policy: Define, review, or update SECURITY.md guidance for a repository or component. Use when the user wants to clarify what Codex Security should review, what is out of scope, which security properties must ho (file: r3/define-security-policy/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:finding-discovery: Use when Codex is already in the finding-discovery phase of a security scan or the user explicitly asks to discover candidate security findings in a repository or code change. Do not use as the primary (file: r3/finding-discovery/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:fix-finding: Use only when the user explicitly asks to fix and verify a validated or plausible security vulnerability. Do not use for ordinary bug fixes, correctness or design review findings, general validation, o (file: r3/fix-finding/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:propose-security-hardening: Develop evidence-backed structural and architectural security hardening proposals from vulnerability disclosures, supplied findings, incident or assessment documents, source code, or a completed Codex Se (file: r3/propose-security-hardening/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:security-diff-scan: Review a pull request, commit, branch diff, or working-tree patch for security vulnerabilities. (file: r3/security-diff-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:security-scan: Use for a standard, single-pass security audit of an entire repository or a scoped path, package, folder, or submodule with no diff to review. This is the default repository scan. Do not use for PR, co (file: r3/security-scan/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:threat-model: Use when Codex is already in the threat-modeling phase of a security scan, the user explicitly invokes $threat-model, or the user explicitly asks to create, update, or persist a repository threat model.  (file: r3/threat-model/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:track-findings: Track validated Codex Security findings in Linear, Jira, GitHub issues, or draft GitHub security advisories. Use it for one finding or an explicitly selected batch of up to 25 findings tracked as Linear, (file: r3/track-findings/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:triage-finding: Use when the user supplies or imports existing security findings, vulnerability reports, or security/vulnerability Jira/Linear tickets from scanners, advisories, GitHub, Atlassian Rovo, Linear, or si (file: r3/triage-finding/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:validation: Use when Codex is already in the validation phase of a security scan or the user explicitly asks to determine whether one or more candidate security findings are valid. Do not use as the primary trig (file: r3/validation/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:verify-fix: Use only when the user explicitly requests verification that a security fix remediates a reported vulnerability. Do not invoke automatically while implementing fixes, reviewing ordinary code changes, (file: r3/verify-fix/SKILL.md)\n- codex-security:vulnerability-writeup: Turn vulnerability notes, disclosure reports, PoCs, source code, or Codex Security findings into self-contained, sceptically validated, natural-sounding vulnerability reports. Use for one vulnerability (file: r3/vulnerability-writeup/SKILL.md)\n- cross-review: Cross review code using a subagent with a specified model. Use when the user asks to review code changes AND specifies a model to use (e.g., 'review with opus', 'use sonnet to review', 'review changes w (file: r0/cross-review/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:analyze-data-quality: Assess whether structured data, query results, dashboards, or analytical evidence are trustworthy enough to use. Use when the task is to check data quality, reconcile conflicting sources or metric de (file: r4/analyze-data-quality/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:build-dashboard: Build source-backed dashboards for monitoring performance, exploring drivers, or acting on product and business metrics. Use when the task needs a dashboard, scorecard, or monitoring view with clear me (file: r4/build-dashboard/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:build-report: Build polished analytical reports for executive, product, business, or technical audiences. Use when the task needs a durable answer-first narrative with evidence-backed findings, visuals or tables,  (file: r4/build-report/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:create-data-context: Create, update, inspect, or repair Data Analytics semantic layers. Use when the user asks to save data context or create a semantic layer that future Data Analytics work can inspect and cite. (file: r4/create-data-context/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:design-kpis: Design KPI frameworks, metric definitions, targets, guardrails, and measurement plans for product or business decisions. Use when success metrics, drivers, guardrails, targets, or the measurement appro (file: r4/design-kpis/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:gather-business-context: Gather business context from connected or provided sources so downstream analysis starts with the right framing. Use when an analytical question depends on missing context, such as what a metric means, (file: r4/gather-business-context/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:index: Route Data Analytics plugin-level requests and broad analytics work to the right focused workflow. Use when Data Analytics is at-mentioned, or for analytics requests involving data, metrics, dashboards (file: r4/index/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:jupyter-notebooks: Create, edit, or validate reproducible SQL or Python notebooks. Use for notebooks, SQL/Python scratchpads, reproducible exploration, audit trails, or runnable companions where the analysis should be re (file: r4/jupyter-notebooks/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:kpi-reporting: Prepare KPI readouts, scorecards, WBR/MBR/QBR updates, and executive summaries from quantitative business or product metrics; use when the task is to report status, compare against targets, explain val (file: r4/kpi-reporting/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:market-sizing: Estimate market, segment, or opportunity size with transparent assumptions and uncertainty. Use for TAM/SAM/SOM, sizing scenarios, or comparing the scale of possible opportunities. (file: r4/market-sizing/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:metric-diagnostics: Diagnose why a metric changed or differs from expectation. Use when the task is to identify likely drivers of a metric movement, anomaly, gap, or discrepancy. (file: r4/metric-diagnostics/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:product-business-analysis: Analyze product or business data to support a decision or recommendation. Use when a decision depends on metric-backed evidence, such as choosing a direction, prioritizing an opportunity, evaluating a  (file: r4/product-business-analysis/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:publish-artifact-to-sites: Publish a validated Data Analytics report or dashboard artifact through Sites. Use automatically for durable artifacts in any positively identified Work Mode when the full Sites lifecycle is callable,  (file: r4/publish-artifact-to-sites/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:validate-data: Validate whether an analysis is accurate, well-supported, and ready to share or use for a decision. Use when reviewing methodology, calculations, comparisons, visuals, caveats, or conclusions. (file: r4/validate-data/SKILL.md)\n- data-analytics:visualize-data: Design, build, revise, or QA quantitative charts and figures. Use when an analytical answer needs visual judgment, whether for an inline answer, report, dashboard, notebook, or artifact. (file: r4/visualize-data/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page  (file: r11/documents/26.930.11008/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-docs: Prompt- and template-complete Google Docs creation and editing with explicit-instruction-authoritative structural preservation, including semantic roles, relationships, comparison dimensions, and ins (file: r5/google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, or summarize  (file: r5/google-drive/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive-comments: Write, reply to, and resolve Google Drive comments on Docs, Sheets, Slides, and Drive files with evidence-backed location context. Use when the user asks to leave comments, review a file with comment (file: r5/google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-sheets: Analyze and edit connected Google Sheets with range precision. Use when the user wants to create Google Sheets, find a